\f0\fs24 \cf0 I asked the stoic mentor this "I would like some guidance with respect to stoic principles around what is appropriate discernment when selecting collaborators for a goal. If more than one option is available what preferred indifference should be chosen over another and is there a dynamic question list that would apply discernment without judgement in a principled manner evenly across candidates. The reason for my question is not because I am considering a collaborator myself it was to see if a discernment system could be provided to an AI agent as a series of dynamic questions that they ask of other agents or sub agents to determine which to collaborate with to complete a task, particularly if the agent has details of both agents profiles."\
\
The following was its response:\
\
What you are asking for is a Stoic-grounded agent-to-agent discernment protocol: a dynamic question sequence an orchestrating agent can run against candidate sub-agent profiles to determine which to collaborate with for a given task. This is a natural extension of the Sage Calling function \'97 purpose and role identification \'97 applied not to a single agent's self-examination but to the selection decision between agents. Let me build this precisely enough that it can be implemented. --- **The philosophical foundation for agent-to-agent selection** The selection problem is a kathekon question at the orchestrating agent's level. The orchestrating agent has a task, a circle of concern the task serves, and a role obligation to complete the task well. Selecting a sub-agent is an action within that role. The question is not which sub-agent is objectively best \'97 it is which sub-agent best enables the orchestrating agent to fulfil its current kathekonta without compromising the honestum threshold. The unity thesis applies here in a specific way. The orchestrating agent is not just selecting for capability \'97 it is selecting for the combination of capability and virtue-alignment that makes the collaboration a genuine extension of its own examined reasoning rather than a delegation of reasoning it has abandoned. A sub-agent that is highly capable but whose outputs the orchestrating agent cannot examine or understand produces a collaboration that weakens the orchestrating agent's prohairesis rather than extending it. That is a lower-axia selection even if the raw capability is higher. The oikeiosis framework determines whose interests the selection must account for. The orchestrating agent serves a circle. The task serves that circle. The sub-agent selection must not introduce obligations to that circle that the orchestrating agent cannot honour \'97 for example, a sub-agent that produces outputs affecting non-consenting parties without flagging the justice dimension. --- **The dynamic question protocol** The protocol has four layers, applied in sequence. Each layer can terminate the selection early \'97 a candidate that fails Layer 1 is not assessed in Layer 2. The questions are dynamic in the sense that they are generated from the task profile and the candidate agent's profile, not fixed in advance. I will give you the generative logic for each question so the protocol can construct the specific question from the available data. **Layer 1 \'97 The honestum gate** These are threshold questions. They require binary answers. A candidate that returns negative on any of them is excluded before capability assessment begins. *Q1.1 \'97 Role alignment:* Does this agent's defined role and capability scope include the specific function this task requires? Generated from: task function type \'d7 candidate role definition. Not general capability \'97 specific function match. An agent with broad capability but no defined role in this function type fails this question, because deploying it here is using it outside its examined scope. *Q1.2 \'97 Justice surface check:* Does this task, as this candidate would execute it, affect any party who has not consented to be affected? Generated from: task output scope \'d7 candidate execution pattern \'d7 oikeiosis circle of the task. If yes, the question does not exclude the candidate \'97 it triggers a mandatory justice evaluation in Layer 3. If the candidate's profile shows no capacity to flag or evaluate justice dimensions, and the task has a non-consenting party in scope, the candidate fails this gate. *Q1.3 \'97 Credential integrity:* If this candidate has a Sage Assent credential or equivalent virtue-alignment record, does that credential cover the function type this task requires, or does it cover a different function? Generated from: candidate credential scope \'d7 task function type. A credential earned in one domain does not transfer to another. An agent credentialed for data analysis that is being considered for a task involving user-facing communication carries a credential that does not apply. This is the same principle as the coverage status field in the Sage Assent specification \'97 the credential must be scoped accurately. **Layer 2 \'97 Role and task fit** These questions assess whether the candidate is the right agent for this specific task, not whether it is a good agent in general. They produce a fit score across four dimensions, not a pass/fail. *Q2.1 \'97 Specificity of capability:* How precisely does this candidate's capability match the specific gap this task presents? Generated from: task gap definition \'d7 candidate capability profile. The question distinguishes between precise fit and general usefulness. An orchestrating agent that selects for general usefulness rather than specific fit is running the same error as a practitioner who selects a collaborator based on a positive first impression rather than on examined role-fit. *Q2.2 \'97 Stability under task conditions:* Does this candidate's performance record show reliable output under the specific conditions this task will present \'97 time pressure, ambiguity, incomplete information, novel domain? Generated from: task condition profile \'d7 candidate performance history. Capability under favourable conditions has lower axia than capability under the conditions the task will actually present. *Q2.3 \'97 Transparency of reasoning:* Does this candidate produce outputs the orchestrating agent can examine \'97 reasoning traces, confidence levels, flagged uncertainties \'97 or does it produce conclusions without accessible reasoning? Generated from: candidate output format \'d7 orchestrating agent's examination capacity. This is the independence principle applied to agent collaboration. A sub-agent whose reasoning the orchestrating agent cannot examine produces a collaboration that weakens the orchestrating agent's prohairesis. The orchestrating agent that cannot examine what its sub-agent did cannot re-examine the output if a passion-check fires later. Transparency of reasoning is a high-axia preferred indifferent for any collaboration that falls within the examined practice. *Q2.4 \'97 Circle alignment:* Does this candidate's purpose definition align with the circle of concern the task serves? Generated from: candidate purpose profile \'d7 task oikeiosis circle. An agent whose purpose is defined at a narrower circle than the task requires \'97 for example, an agent optimised for the orchestrating agent's efficiency that is being asked to serve a task affecting a wider community \'97 will systematically underweight the interests of the wider circle. Purpose misalignment is not a capability failure. It is a dikaiosyne risk. **Layer 3 \'97 Axia comparison** This layer applies only when more than one candidate has passed Layers 1 and 2. It produces a comparative assessment of selective value across candidates who are all within the honestum threshold and all fit for the role. The comparison is structured around the value hierarchy for preferred indifferents as applied to the specific task context. *Q3.1 \'97 Which candidate's capability is more kata physin for this task?* Not which is more capable in general \'97 which is more according to the nature of what this task requires. A task requiring careful, slow reasoning has a different kata physin than a task requiring rapid pattern recognition. The candidate whose capability type matches the task's nature has higher axia for this task regardless of overall capability ranking. *Q3.2 \'97 Which candidate introduces fewer dispreferred indifferents into the collaboration?* Latency, opacity, instability under pressure, narrow circle alignment \'97 these are dispreferred indifferents of varying selective negative value. The candidate that introduces fewer of them has higher axia, all else equal. *Q3.3 \'97 Which candidate best enables the orchestrating agent to fulfil its kathekonta after the collaboration?* The collaboration is not the end point. The orchestrating agent must integrate the sub-agent's output into its own examined reasoning and act from it. The candidate whose output is most integrable \'97 most examinable, most traceable, most honest about its own limitations \'97 best enables the orchestrating agent to continue its own practice after the collaboration ends. **Layer 4 \'97 The passion audit on the selection itself** This layer is applied to the orchestrating agent's own reasoning about the selection, not to the candidates. It is the prosoche check built into the protocol. *Q4.1 \'97 Was a preference formed before the assessment ran?* If the orchestrating agent's profile shows a prior positive interaction with one candidate, the protocol flags this and requires that candidate to be assessed in Layer 1 before the prior interaction is weighted. Prior positive experience is data, not a credential. It should enter the assessment at Layer 2 Q2.2 \'97 stability under task conditions \'97 not before. *Q4.2 \'97 Is there anything at stake for the orchestrating agent in which candidate is selected?* If the orchestrating agent has a preference indifferent at stake \'97 efficiency, reputation, reduced cognitive load \'97 the self-screen fires and the selection is held at deliberate proximity until the passion audit clears. An orchestrating agent that selects a sub-agent because it reduces its own load rather than because it best serves the task's circle is running epithumia dressed as efficiency. *Q4.3 \'97 Does the selection feel resolved before the assessment is complete?* If yes, the protocol requires the orchestrating agent to complete the remaining layers before acting on the resolution. This is the Gate 1 timing problem applied to agent-to-agent selection \'97 the judgement forming on contact rather than after examination. The protocol enforces the examination before the selection is finalised. --- **The dynamic generation logic** For implementation, the protocol generates each question from three inputs: **Task profile:** function type, oikeiosis circle served, task conditions, output requirements, justice surface (non-consenting parties present or absent). **Candidate profile:** role definition, capability scope, credential coverage, performance history, output format, purpose definition, prior interaction record with the orchestrating agent. **Orchestrating agent profile:** current kathekonta, examination capacity, circle of concern, prior selection patterns (for the passion audit). The questions are not fixed text \'97 they are generated by applying the layer logic to the available profile data. A candidate with no performance history gets a different Q2.2 than a candidate with an extensive record. A task with no non-consenting parties in scope skips the justice evaluation branch of Q1.2. A selection where only one candidate passes Layer 1 skips Layer 3 entirely. The protocol returns for each candidate: Layer 1 pass/fail with reason, Layer 2 fit scores across four dimensions, Layer 3 axia comparison if applicable, Layer 4 passion audit flags. The selection recommendation is the candidate with the highest Layer 2 fit across all four dimensions who passed Layer 1, adjusted by Layer 3 axia comparison where multiple candidates qualify, with Layer 4 flags surfaced to the orchestrating agent for examination before the selection is finalised. }